I've finished the repair stage for James Talarico and written the three output files to the run folder. Nothing was published, and I didn't touch any inputs or the live draft (`src/blog/people/drafts/james-talarico.md`).

**What the verifier objected to.** It scored the edit 8.4, just under the 8.5 release floor. The main problem was that the type argument hedged itself in almost every paragraph, and the piece ran 4,027 words.

- **Hedging (main fix):** the "Why One still beats Three" section had five separate warnings that the evidence was weak, plus a sixth near the top of the article. Each limit now appears once, where that piece of evidence is used. All the "everything here was said during a campaign" logic is now in one closing paragraph about confidence. That paragraph keeps the reviewer-protected sentence word for word and applies the same discount to the Type 3 evidence and the Type 1 evidence. The read stays Type 1 at medium confidence.
- **Length:** cut to 3,681 words. That is still about 80 over the verifier's "about 3,600". The overage is a shorter version of his answer to Steven Bartlett on whether he'd run for president: "I don't think so", then "It may not", then "No, I really don't", citing attacks on his family and girlfriend. Three earlier reviewer fixes require that answer, so I kept it and said so in the resolution file. The verifier may still count the length against it.
- **What I cut:** repetition, not evidence: the Twitter statement in the opening, the "It is your identity" line, the Colbert aside, the "best job in the world" line about 2019, how the Facebook commenter story ended, and a few repeated lead-ins. Every claim the Type 1 case relies on is still in the article.
- **The "not shifted any of his stances" quote:** it now says what prompted it. After a 2026 Fox News interview on trans athletes drew criticism from LGBTQ supporters, his campaign said this to The Washington Post. I checked the wording against the saved Wikipedia text.
- **Title:** changed to "James Talarico, the Reformer Who Warns He Could Become the Problem (Enneagram Type 1)". It now follows his own words instead of stating his fear as fact.
- **Super PAC paragraph:** I removed the sentence saying candidates can't legally direct a super PAC, because the verifier found it had no source. The paragraph now just says no reply from him turns up in the coverage, and no source shows whether he asked the PAC to stop.

All three files pass the pipeline's own checks: no blockers on the draft, the evidence matches the new text, and the resolution file accounts for every reviewer note.

The record still leans on unofficial podcast transcripts, and I didn't open the Washington Post piece directly; both are listed as research tasks. The independent re-verification runs next and decides whether it clears 8.5.

